  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nNighttime satellite imagery at the time of writing depicts\nblanketing stratus entrenched over the forecast area still this\nmorning. Embedded drizzle and mountain flurries, evident still on\nlocal traffic cameras, will become compressed by later this\nmorning as low level moisture grows shallow. \n\nDry air will then move into the region in earnest under the \nguise of building high pressure. This feature, coupled with\nupper level ridging aloft, will take center stage over the\nCentral Appalachians through the course of the work week. Amid\nits residency over the area, dry weather will rule the roost\nalongside warming temperatures. A daily increase in temperature\nswings will transpire, with diurnal heating yielding above\nnormal afternoon highs and strong radiational cooling overnight\npromoting morning lows dropping down into the 20s/30s. \n\nThermal profiles produced by forecast soundings for this week \nshows vast amounts of dry air bestowed upon the area by the\nsurface high and upper ridge. Coupled with above normal daytime\ntemperatures, minimum RH values may drop as low as 20 to 30\npercent beginning Tuesday afternoon along the WV mountains and\nfoothills. While the wind component will be lacking to suggest\nfire weather concerns, this will remain closely monitored each\nday for potential heightened messaging.\n\nOtherwise, next opportunity for precipitation on the horizon\ncould arrive by the weekend once the upper level ridge breaks \ndown and a frontal boundary slides across the country.",
